+</div>
+<div class="note">The soft shadows are best seen in the full frames: 33%→16% alpha ink blobs, oversized + closer to the footprint (the UE “soft blob” recipe) — gentle depth separation, never a hard drop shadow.</div>
+
+<h2>4 · The UE slice-1 reference (the bar)</h2>
+<div class="ue">
+  <div class="frame"><img src="frames/ue-slice1-2-drawn-pipe.png" alt="UE slice1-2"><div class="cap">UE slice-1 — source → router → sink: cream paper, white ribbon with a hairline casing, two-tone node discs.</div></div>
+  <div class="frame"><img src="frames/ue-slice1-3-packet.png" alt="UE slice1-3"><div class="cap">UE slice-1 — packet mid-traversal: the eased motion read. (Our pipes keep the tier colors — the tier is load-bearing canon; we match the FEEL, not the fixture.)</div></div>
+</div>
+<div class="note">UE frames read-only from <code>packet-plumber-ue/docs/goldens/screens/</code> (the look-parity checklist). UE canvas ≈ #F2EEE3; ours lifts #E8DDC2 → #EDE2C8 — a deliberate partial step (the landmass + grid contrast ride our canvas too).</div>
+
+<h2>5 · What changed (5 reviewable commits + pins)</h2>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Commit</th><th>Change</th><th>Pixel proof</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>1 · soft shadows</td><td>Node contact shadows 33%→16% alpha ink, ellipse 0.56×0.33→0.64×0.36 of the footprint, offset 0.10/0.24→0.06/0.12 (UE “oversized low-alpha blobs”)</td><td>juice 30000ms: 3694/921600 px — shadow footprints only</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>2 · ribbon casing</td><td>Darker opaque under-stroke under every pipe band (rim ~2.25px/side, round caps); tier color darkened 22% toward ink — OPAQUE on purpose (rlsw line-alpha trap)</td><td>5548 px differ — 5391 of them EXACTLY the three predicted casing blends (copper/steel/fiber)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>3 · canvas lift</td><td>#E8DDC2 → #EDE2C8 warm paper (UE step); settings chip stops hardcoding the hex; look-book amendment 8</td><td>palcheck 38/38 (map pins re-pinned); a11y oracle green — mode tables untouched</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>4 · packet glint</td><td>Small warm-cream highlight up-left of circular dots; alpha follows the focus-ghost dim; triangles untouched</td><td>diff = glint color only (139–143 px/frame ≈ packets × glint area)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>5 · unit pins</td><td>casing blend exact values + shipped canvas token; a pre-existing 12B palette-load leak fixed (json parse tree never destroyed)</td><td>13/13 render tests green</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>6 · gate r1</td><td>STREAM GLINT (triangles get the same highlight) + the MM-ward sprite touch-up: thin canon-ink outline plates under every terminal + flattened wall bands (the extruded-2.5D read → painted base)</td><td>juice 30000ms +6633 px: 362 pure ink outline + the wall-tone shift; palcheck 38/38 (pins unchanged — the roof-panel tones still emit)</td></tr>
+</table>
+<div class="note">
+  <span class="pill">T1: all 45 demos’ .log.bin + .t1 byte-identical to HEAD</span>
+  <span class="pill">CI: tools/ci-local.sh --mac 10/10 green</span>
+  <span class="pill">Perf: juice 0.86→0.85s · estate_surge 0.59→0.60s · qos_contention 0.21→0.21s (medians, 3 runs — noise)</span>
+  <span class="pill">Re-bless: deliberate, per commit, cause-documented</span>
+</div>
+
+<div class="ask">
+  <h3>Your round-1 notes — addressed</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li><b>“the stream might need the same touch up”</b> — done: the streaming triangles now carry the same warm-cream glint (just below the apex).</li>
+    <li><b>“touch up the router/terminal graphics … why can’t we get that look or something close to that? why is it hard?”</b> — done + honest answer below.</li>
+  </ul>
+  <h3>Why the MM look felt far (and what closes the gap)</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li>MM’s look is hand-crafted flat vector art: <b>one fill + a thin dark outline + zero extrusion</b>. Our sprites were Blender-rendered with a tilt, so walls showed as <b>pseudo-3D extruded bands</b>, and every edge was a tone change instead of a line.</li>
+    <li>It’s not hard — it’s iterative. The pipeline is parametric + data-driven (regenerate → re-bless → look), so each pass is cheap. This pass: <b>thin canon-ink outlines</b> (MM’s defining edge) + <b>flattened wall bands</b>. The canon details stay on purpose (the play-marking, LED rings, roof tones — the 7.1 gate approved them and palcheck pins them).</li>
+    <li>The honest residual gap: MM buildings sit on near-blank paper; ours sit on a busy map (grid + water + parks — canon D9). The outlines + flatter bases are what closes most of the perceived gap from here.</li>
+  </ul>
+</div>
+
+<div class="ask">
+  <h3>Judge with your eyes — annotate anything</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li>Do the terminals read closer to MM now (outline + flat base)? Too much outline / too little?</li>
+    <li>Does the stream glint look right? Should the packets keep theirs?</li>
+    <li>Is the ribbon casing too strong or too subtle? Are the shadows soft enough?</li>
+    <li>Anything that looks WORSE than before — say so; the pass must never regress the 7.1 base.</li>
+  </ul>
+  <div class="note">Select any image or text and queue your note. <b>Send &amp; End</b> = approve this direction → the PR opens.</div>
+</div>
